e office, then they visit the office supply store regularly).\n     - \\( R \\rightarrow \\neg (P \\land \\neg R) \\) (If Remy spends their free time tidying and organizing the spaces in their home, then they do not go to bed late and wake up every day after noon).\n\n4. **Analyzing the Statement:**\n   - The statement to evaluate is: \\( (P \\lor Q) \\rightarrow (\\neg R \\land \\neg S) \\).\n\n5. **Using Logical Implications:**\n   - From \\( P \\rightarrow R \\) and \\( Q \\rightarrow S \\), we can infer:\n     - \\( \\neg R \\rightarrow \\neg P \\) (Contrapositive of \\( P \\rightarrow R \\)).\n     - \\( \\neg S \\rightarrow \\neg Q \\) (Contrapositive of \\( Q \\rightarrow S \\)).\n\n6. **Evaluating the Statement:**\n   - Assume \\( P \\lor Q \\) is true.\n   - We need to show that \\( \\neg R \\land \\neg S \\) must be true.\n\n7. **Case Analysis:**\n   - **Case 1: \\( P \\) is true.**\n     - If \\( P \\) is true, then \\( R \\) is true (from \\( P \\rightarrow R \\)).\n     - This contradicts \\( \\neg R \\) in \\( \\neg R \\land \\neg S \\).\n   - **Case 2: \\( Q \\) is true.**\n     - If \\( Q \\) is true, then \\( S \\) is true (from \\( Q \\rightarrow S \\)).\n     - This contradicts \\( \\neg S \\) in \\( \\neg R \\land \\neg S \\).\n\n8. **Conclusion:**\n   - Since both cases lead to contradictions, the statement \\( (P \\lor Q) \\rightarrow (\\neg R \\land \\neg S) \\) must be false.\n\nTherefore, the statement is **False**.\n</think>\n<answer>\nB) False\n</answer>",
